<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch10-section-thermal-wind" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The thermal wind</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We can integrate <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-wind-shear-vector" text="type-global" />
      with respect to pressure <m>p</m> between pressure levels <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m>,
      where <m>p_1 \gt p_2</m>.
      As with our
      <xref ref="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-hypsometric-equation" text="custom"> derivation of the hypsometric equation in Chapter 7</xref>,
      we can replace <m>T</m> with the pressure-weighted column-average temperature <m>\overline{T}</m>,
      which is a constant.
      Then this integration produces
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" number="yes">
          \vec{v}_T = \vec{v}_g(p_2) - \vec{v}_g(p_1)
          =
          \frac{R_d}{f}\ln{\left( \frac{p_1}{p_2} \right)} \hat{k} \times \nabla_p \overline{T}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
      gives the <term>thermal wind</term> <m>\vec{v}_T</m>
      and reveals <m>\vec{v}_T</m>
      is the vector difference between the geostrophic wind measured at two pressure levels of interest <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m>.
      In other words,
      the thermal wind thus defined is the <term>bulk vertical shear of the geostrophic wind</term>,
      where "bulk" means the shear is measured between two pressure levels.
      Furthermore,
      <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
      reveals the thermal wind is directly proportional to the horizontal pressure-weighted column-average temperature gradient
      measured at a pressure level of interest.
    </p>

    <p>
      The vector form of the thermal wind <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
      produces the following thermal wind components:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-x" number="yes">
          u_T
          =
          -\frac{R_d}{f} \ln{\left( \frac{p_1}{p_2} \right)}
          \left( \frac{\partial \overline{T}}{\partial y} \right)_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-y" number="yes">
          v_T
          =
          \frac{R_d}{f} \ln{\left( \frac{p_1}{p_2} \right)}
          \left( \frac{\partial \overline{T}}{\partial x} \right)_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Moving the quantity <m>R_d \ln{\left( \dfrac{p_1}{p_2} \right)}</m>
      in <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
      into the gradient
      and taking advantage of the form of the hypsometric equation in terms of geopotential <m>\Phi</m>
      <xref ref="eqn-hypsometric-equation-geopotential" text="type-global" />
      produces an alternative form of the thermal wind equation in terms of geopotential thickness <m>\Delta \Phi</m>
      between pressure levels <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential" number="yes">
          \vec{v}_T
          =
          \vec{v}_g(p_2) - \vec{v}_g(p_1)
          =
          \frac{1}{f} \hat{k} \times \nabla_p (\Delta \Phi)
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      with components
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-geopotential-x" number="yes">
          u_T
          =
          -\frac{1}{f} \left( \frac{\partial (\Delta \Phi)}{\partial y} \right)_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-geopotential-y" number="yes">
          v_T
          =
          \frac{1}{f} \left( \frac{\partial (\Delta \Phi)}{\partial x} \right)_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly,
      multiplying and dividing <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector" text="type-global" />
      by <m>g_0</m>,
      moving the quantity <m>\dfrac{R_d}{g_0} \ln{\left( \dfrac{p_1}{p_2} \right)}</m> into the gradient,
      and taking advantage of the form of the hypsometric equation in terms of geopotential height <m>Z</m>
      <xref ref="eqn-hypsometric-equation-geopotential-height" text="type-global" />
      produces another alternative form of the thermal wind equation,
      this time in terms of geopotential height thickness <m>\Delta Z</m>
      between pressure levels <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential-height" number="yes">
          \vec{v}_T
          =
          \vec{v}_g(p_2) - \vec{v}_g(p_1)
          =
          \frac{g_0}{f} \hat{k} \times \nabla_p (\Delta Z)
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      with components
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-geopotential-height-x" number="yes">
          u_T
          =
          -\frac{g_0}{f} \left( \frac{\partial (\Delta Z)}{\partial y} \right)_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-thermal-wind-geopotential-height-y" number="yes">
          v_T
          =
          \frac{g_0}{f} \left( \frac{\partial (\Delta Z)}{\partial x} \right)_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential" text="type-global" />
      and  <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-vector-geopotential-height" text="type-global" />
      reveal the thermal wind is directly proportional to the horizontal thickness gradient measured at a pressure level of interest.
      Furthermore,
      these equations reveal the thermal wind flows parallel to thickness contours for two pressure levels of interest,
      similar to how <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-balance-vector" text="type-global" />
      reveals the local vertical shear of the geostrophic wind is parallel to isotherms contoured on an isobaric surface of interest.
      An example for the Northern Hemisphere of the thermal wind flowing parallel to geopotential height thickness contours
      is shown in <xref ref="ch10-thermal-wind" text="type-global" /> below.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch10-thermal-wind">
      <caption>
          The 552 dam and 558 dam contours of 1000<ndash />500 hPa thickness
          are contoured on a 1000<ndash />500 hPa thickness map for the Northern Hemisphere.
          At the location between the contours marked by the circle,
          the 1000<ndash />500 hPa thickness gradient is indicated by the vector labeled <m>\nabla_p (\Delta Z)</m>,
          the <m>\hat{k}</m> vector points out of the screen,
          and the thermal wind depicted by the vector labeled <m>\vec{v}_T</m>
          points parallel to the thickness contours with smaller thickness to its left.
          Note <m>\vec{v}_T</m> gives the bulk shear of <m>\vec{v}_g</m> between 1000 hPa and 500 hPa,
          i.e., <m>\vec{v}_T = \vec{v}_{g,500 \, \text{hPa}} - \vec{v}_{g,1000 \, \text{hPa}}</m>.
      </caption>
      <image source="images/ch10-thermal-wind.png" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
              The vector representing the thermal wind points parallel to two thickness contours on an isobaric surface
              with smaller thickness to its left in the Northern Hemisphere.
              This means the vector representing the thermal wind
              is 90° to the left of the horizontal geopotential height thickness gradient vector on the isobaric surface.
          </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="ch10-section-thermal-wind-subsection-temperature-advection.ptx" />

</section>